+ * giving a false positive of (60/256)**6 == 0.0001
+ *
+ * We choose k = 6, rather than a higher number as
+ * it means the false positive rate grows slower for high n.
+ *
+ * n = 5, k = 6 => fp = 2.6e-6
+ * n = 5, k = 8 => fp = 3.5e-7
+ * n = 10, k = 6 => fp = 1.6e-4
+ * n = 10, k = 8 => fp = 0.9e-4
+ * n = 15, k = 6 => fp = 0.18%
+ * n = 15, k = 8 => fp = 0.23%
+ * n = 20, k = 6 => fp = 1.1%
+ * n = 20, k = 8 => fp = 2.3%
